# labwc session environment for the Smart First Aid Box cabinet kiosk.
#
# STATUS: NEVER APPLIED. Not read by labwc on hardware.
# Install: /home/technology/.config/labwc/environment
# Format: KEY=value, one per line, no `export`, no shell expansion.
#
# Kept almost empty on purpose. Everything in this file affects the whole session, so a
# wrong value here fails at login with no UI to report it — and on a locked-down kiosk,
# a session that will not start is a service call, not an inconvenience. Add lines only
# when you have a symptom that needs one.

# Keyboard layout for the session. Set it explicitly rather than inheriting whatever the
# image defaulted to: the cabinet normally has no keyboard, so a wrong layout stays
# invisible until the day someone plugs one in to fix something and finds the keys are
# not where the labels say.
XKB_DEFAULT_LAYOUT=us

# ── Considered and deliberately NOT set ──────────────────────────────────────
#
# The mouse pointer stays visible. The touch device is configured with
# mouseEmulation=yes (that setting is part of the working touch configuration and must
# not be dropped), so a pointer will sit wherever the last tap landed. There is no
# supported labwc option to hide it, and the usual answers do not apply cleanly:
#   · `unclutter` / `unclutter-xfixes` are X11 tools and do not see Wayland surfaces
#   · a fully transparent XCURSOR_THEME works but means shipping a cursor theme and
#     makes the pointer invisible in maintenance sessions too
# Left unsolved and listed in ../README.md as an open cosmetic item rather than
# pretending one of the above is a fix. Confirm on the device how distracting it
# actually is before spending anything on it.
#
# GDK_SCALE / GDK_DPI_SCALE are not set here. Display scaling for the browser is handled
# by --force-device-scale-factor=1 in chromium-kiosk-flags.conf, in one place. Setting
# both is how you end up with text that is right in one view and wrong in another.
